                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+covering+spaces"} fundamental theorem of covering spaces
            says that for a sufficiently nice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            at a point can be reconstructed as a group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deck+transformations"} deck transformations
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+covering+space"} universal covering space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            over that point of the projection map. The deck transformations are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monodromies"} monodromies
            induced by loops at the base point.
          %p
            The functor which assigns to a point the fiber over it, generalizes to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functors"} fiber functors
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannakian formalism
            of Grothendieck which defines in more general setups the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            as the group of automorphisms of the appropriate fiber functor. See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group+of+a+topos"} fundamental group of a topos
          %p
            Now, Grothendieck’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            means to generalize to the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            this familiar correspondence between
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+spaces"} covering spaces
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            \:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} -sets
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+path-connected+space"} locally path connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-locally+simply+connected+space"} semilocally simply connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The objects on the left are not difficult to define for schemes (at least naively – one really needs trivialisations over étale
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} covers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            defined in terms of loops should be.
          %p
            The reason Galois’s name is attached to this theory is that in the case of the scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(k)
            associated to a field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(k')
            for
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+extension"} field extension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(k)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+group"} Galois group
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+fundamental+group"} algebraic fundamental group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite group
          %h3#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            The basic idea of Grothendieck’s Galois theory may be extended to objects in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            – leading to a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group+of+a+topos"} fundamental group of a topos
            – and then further to objects in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group+of+an+%E2%88%9E-stack"} homotopy group of an ∞-stack
          %h2#details Details
          %h3#preliminaries Preliminaries
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Given an arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:x \to y
              in a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  category of arrows compatible with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(f)
              is the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undercategory"} undercategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \downarrow C
              on the arrows that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalize
              the same pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi w
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,h:w\rightrightarrows x
              that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              does.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:x\to y
              in a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+epimorphism"} strict epimorphism
              if it is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Comp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(f)
          %p It is not obvious, but a strict epimorphism is an epimorphism.
          %h3#grothendiecks_axioms Grothendieck’s axioms
          %p
            In what follows, Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C \to Set
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %p
            J. P. Murre,
            = succeed "," do
              %em Lectures on an introduction to Grothendieck’s theory of the fundamental group
          %p and copied also in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
              , C. S. de la Vega
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Galois theory of Grothendieck
              %strong 65
              (2000) 111–136.
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0009145"} arXiv
          %p
            Some terminology:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in C
            is called
            %em finite
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(X)
            is a finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi F
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_F C
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,a)
            is called finite if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is finite.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong G0)
                The full subcategory of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi F
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_F C
                on the finite objects is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofinal+subcategory"} cofinal
            %li
              %p
                %strong G1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                has all finite
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            %li
              %p
                %strong G2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                has an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+object"} quotients
                by finite
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %li
              %p
                %strong G3)
                Given
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:x\to z
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                there is a factorisation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi e
                      %mi y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi i
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{e}{\to} y \stackrel{i}{\to} z
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                is a strict epimorphism and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                is a mono. Also,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                is assumed to be a
                = succeed "mand" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} direct sum
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %li
              %p
                %strong G4)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                preserves finite limits
            %li
              %p
                %strong G5)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                preserves initial object, finite sums, quotients by finite group actions and sends strict epimorphisms to surjections
            %li
              %p
                %strong G6)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                reflects isomorphisms
          %hr/
          %p
            The functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em fibre functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,F)
            is sometimes called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em Galois category
          %p
            It follows from the axioms that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-representable+functor"} pro-representable functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            representing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is (should be. I’m not familiar enough with pro-objects) a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(X) = [P,-]
            by precomposition (talking out of my depth here – it’s getting a bit vague) and so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            lifts to a functor to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo −
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi-Set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %p
            There are several modifications one can make the above. In the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the category of covering spaces of a nice enough space, the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+covering+space"} universal covering space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+action"} transitive
            = succeed "-sets." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %h2#the_classical_case_of_fields The classical case of fields
          %p
            Even for the classical case of the inclusion of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \subset L
            be a finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+extension"} Galois extension
            of fields. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L : K]
            denote the group of
            = succeed "-automorphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo −
                  %mi finSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal [L : K]-finSet
            the category of finite
            = succeed "-sets." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gal
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L : K]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SplitfinAlg
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SplitfinAlg_K(L)
            denote the finite dimensional
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            itself is an object. Consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi SplitfinAlg
                      %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_L = Hom_K(-,L):SplitfinAlg_K(L)\to Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of finite sets and it comes with a canonical
            = succeed "-action." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gal
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L : K]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gal
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo −
                    %mi finSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal [L : K]-finSet
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SplitfinAlg
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo −
                  %mi finSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  SplitfinAlg_K(L)\to Gal [L : K]-finSet
          %p
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %p There is an infinitary version as well, generalizing the classical Galois theorem on infinitary Galois extensions.
          %p
            Thus let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\subset L
            be an arbitrary Galois extension. Now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L:K]
            denotes the profinite Galois group and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo −
                  %mi profinSpace
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L:K]-profinSpace
            the category or profinite
            = succeed "-spaces." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gal
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal[L:K]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SplitAlg
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SplitAlg_K(L)
            denotes the category of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            (possible infinite-dimensional). Then there is a canonical anti-equivalence of categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SplitAlg
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Gal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo −
                  %mi profinSpace
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  SplitAlg_K(L)\to Gal [L : K]-profinSpace
          %p
            (factorizing a profinite-space version of the representable functor
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_K(-,L)
          %p
            A special case of this is the following: the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+scheme"} étale k-schemes
            reps.
            %span.newWikiWord
              étale group schemes
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%C3%A9tale+group+scheme"} ?
            for a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is equivalent to the category of sets equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+Galois+group"} absolute Galois group
            reps. to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+module"} Galois modules
            of the absolute Galois group.
          %h2#galois_theorem_for_locales_and_topoi Galois theorem for locales and topoi
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+groupoid"} localic groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is equivalent to the category of étale presheaves over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#JoyalTierney84"} Joyal &amp; Tierney 1984
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+of+a+localic+groupoid"} classifying topos of a localic groupoid
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+of+a+localic+groupoid"} classifying topos of a localic groupoid
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original development of the theory by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , (1971),
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA1"} SGA1
                  – Revetements étales et groupe fondamental
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J. P. Murre,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on an introduction to Grothendieck’s theory of the fundamental group
                = succeed "," do
                  %em 40
            %li#JoyalTierney84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Myles+Tierney"} Myles Tierney
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An extension of the Galois theory of Grothendieck
                %strong 309
                (1984) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/memo-51-309"}> ISBN:978-1-4704-0722-3
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A more recent treatment can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
              , C. S. de la Vega,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Galois theory of Grothendieck
              %strong 65
              (2000) 111–136.
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0009145"} arXiv:math.CT/0009145
          %p and more related categorical and topos theoretic aspects in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Localic Galois theory
              = succeed ":1" do
                %strong 175
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0001-8708(02)00046-4"} doi
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the representation theory of Galois and atomic topoi
              = succeed ":3" do
                %strong 186
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0022-4049(03)00141-5"} doi
          %p A very approachable account is given in
          %ul
            %li
              Marco Antònio Delgado Robalo,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Galois theory towards dessins d’enfants
              %a{:href => "https://fenix.tecnico.ulisboa.pt/downloadFile/395139415315/dissertacao.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            (This has the advantage of looking towards Grothendieck’s
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/children%27s+drawing"} dessins d'enfants
          %p Some basic intuitions are explained in
          %ul
            %li
              Pierre Cartier,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A mad day’s work: from Grothendieck to Connes and Kontsevich The evolution of concepts of space and symmetry
              %strong 38
              (2001), 389-408,
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bull/2001-38-04/S0273-0979-01-00913-2/S0273-0979-01-00913-2.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The construction for general
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topos theory
          %p and, a current state-of-the-art description is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"}> Marta Bunge
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Galois groupoids and covering morphisms in topos theory
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 43
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.15.7071"} links
          %p
            A modern approach from classical via Grothendieck up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+Galois+theory"} categorical Galois theory
            based on precategories and adjunctions is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Janelidze"} George Janelidze
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+Theories"} Galois Theories
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 72
          %p
            The application of a general Tannakian theorem of Saavaedra Rivano, as corrected by Deligne, to the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+Galois+theory"}> differential Galois theory
            ” for differential instead of algebraic equations is in the last chapter of Deligne’s
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat%C3%A9gories+Tannakiennes"} Catégories Tannakiennes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Janelidze"}> George Janelidze
              , Dietmar Schumacher,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
              = succeed "," do
                %em Galois theory in variable categories
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 1
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00872989"} 10.1007/BF00872989
            %li
              Federico G. Lastaria,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On separable algebras in Grothendieck Galois theory
              %a{:href => "http://www.dmi.unict.it/ojs/index.php/lematematiche/article/view/464/0"} link
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
